## Context
Twice in raid 1229 the verify-adherence seat produced an artifact that `adherence_verdict.py` could not read, and gaze escalated or needed a hand repair although the seat itself said PASS:
- PR #1696 (ticket 1230), first gaze: the artifact carried a prose `semantic_review`; adherence exited 2, simplify never ran, gaze ESCALATE.
- PR #1702 (ticket 1232), gaze round 2: invalid YAML from an unquoted colon; gaze re-quoted one line by hand to get PASS.
- PR #1699 (ticket 1233): the semantic phase returned before finishing and was re-run by hand.
Each cost a review round or a manual step, and a run that cannot tell "the seat failed" from "the parser failed" reports a tooling fault as a review verdict.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The seat writes its artifact in a form the parser accepts by construction (structured emit, or schema-validated with a retry), not free YAML
- [ ] A parse failure is reported as a tooling fault distinct from a verdict, and never escalates a PR whose seat returned PASS without saying so
- [ ] Fixtures for the three observed shapes (prose semantic_review, unquoted colon, truncated semantic phase) are handled by test

## Raid annotation (Imagine+Plan, 2026-10-27)
- Premise check: HOLDS on current tree. `scripts/adherence_verdict.py` last touched by 1173 (13b76863); no later commit changed `load`/`validate_seat`. `validate_seat` still raises on a non-mapping `semantic_review` (test_structurally_bad_seat_is_unreadable pins exit 2), `load` calls bare `yaml.safe_load` (unquoted colon -> YAMLError -> exit 2), and a truncated worker file also exits 2. Exit 2 is shared by "tooling" and "bad usage" and carries no seat verdict. No PREMISE-OBJECTION.
- Simplest path (no new module, no new lib; PyYAML already there):
  1. Parser side: on seat/worker load failure, salvage only the top-level `adherence:` scalar by regex, print `tooling_fault: {stage: seat|worker, error, seat_said: PASS|FAIL|null}` as YAML and exit 3 (distinct from verdict 0/1 and usage 2). Never print `adherence: FAIL` for a tooling fault.
  2. Worker side: an unreadable/truncated worker report is `status: invalid` (or `unavailable` if absent) inside `merge`, i.e. the existing `adherence-infrastructure` FAIL, not exit 2. Its reason names the parse error.
  3. Seat side (criterion 1, "by construction"): add `--check-seat FILE` mode (parse + validate_seat, exit 0/2); SKILL.md § 4 tells the seat to run it on its artifact and rewrite once on failure. Coerce a prose `semantic_review` string into `{applicable: true, status: delegated, reason: <string>}` in `validate_seat`'s caller rather than rejecting it (cheap, matches seat intent).
  4. SKILL.md § 3 `semantic=caller` + gaze docs: exit 3 means "re-run seat once, then report tooling fault", not ESCALATE.
- Antipattern scan: YAGNI -> no JSON-schema lib, no emit-DSL; reject a schema-validated retry framework, `--check-seat` plus one SKILL sentence suffices. Do not grow regex salvage beyond the one `adherence:` line (premature abstraction).
- Actions/files: scripts/adherence_verdict.py; tests/test_adherence_verdict.py; skills/verify-adherence/SKILL.md (§ 3, § 4); gaze exit-code handling (skills/gaze/SKILL.md or references/, locate with `grep -rn adherence_verdict skills/gaze`); run `make skills-catalog` only if frontmatter changes (not expected). Deps: none; related closed 1173/1225.
- First failing test: `test_unquoted_colon_seat_reports_tooling_fault`: seat file text `adherence: PASS\nsemantic_review:\n  reason: a: b\n`, assert `main(...) == 3` and capsys output has `tooling_fault` with `seat_said: PASS` and no `adherence: FAIL`. Then fixtures: prose `semantic_review` (assert normalised, exit 0/1 by merge), truncated worker YAML (assert exit 1, status invalid, escalation adherence-infrastructure, not 2).
- Tautology check: the tests run real file text through `main`, assert exit code AND emitted keys; the old code returns 2 so each fails before the change. The `seat_said` assertion would catch an implementation that merely renumbers the exit. Avoid asserting only on `av.merge` with pre-parsed dicts (would miss the YAML layer).
- Shared-file note: low overlap with 1227/1239/1246/1244/1240. Only risk is gaze skill/reference text (1227 gaze setup/closing scripts) and test_gaze_intent.py marker checks; keep the gaze edit to one sentence and rebase before push. No shared script with the others.

## Raid feasibility (2026-10-27)
BLOCK (premise expired since filing; author ruling needed). Commit 5cb969f5 (ticket 1225, 08:39 today) removed the adherence seat from gaze; its message says `adherence_verdict.py`, `agents/adherence-seat.md`, its profile and verify-adherence `semantic=caller` "lose their only caller; their removal is left for a follow-up". `grep -rln semantic=caller` finds only the SKILL and the script. The three incidents (#1696, #1702, #1699) all predate 5cb969f5, so no live path can escalate a gaze on a parse failure.
- Plan step 4 (gaze docs: exit 3) would fail `tests/test_gaze_intent.py::test_gaze_launches_no_verify_adherence` (it forbids `adherence_verdict` in gaze texts).
- Hardening a caller-less script is YAGNI. Options: (a) retarget to the removal follow-up 5cb969f5 names (delete script, test, seat agent/profile, `semantic=caller`; check `tests/test_agent_profiles.py`, `test_profile_tool_grants.py`), about 0.5 coder-hour; (b) close as overtaken by 1225. Recommend (a).
- Smallest check before any work: `git grep -n "adherence_verdict\|semantic=caller\|adherence-seat" -- skills scripts agents profiles`.
